ashboard | Dashboard | P0 |\n67\t| BRANCH-B-E003 | Llama Stack RAG provider wiring for registered external connections | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | Llama Stack | P1 |\n68\t| BRANCH-B-E004 | Product documentation for the external-connection posture | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Documentation | Documentation | P1 |\n69\t\n70\tMain-plan epic count: 1. Total including branch epics: 11. Main-plan critical\n71\tpath length: 1 (E001 alone). Longest path through a branch: 5\n72\t(E001 → BRANCH-A-E003 → BRANCH-A-E005 → BRANCH-A-E006 → BRANCH-A-E008).\n73\t\n74\t## Dependency DAG\n75\t\n76\t```mermaid\n77\tgraph TD\n78\t    E001[\"E001 Posture decision (Investigation)\"]\n79\t\n80\t    subgraph BranchA[\"Branch A — platform-managed\"]\n81\t        AE002[\"A-E002 repo-onboarding\"]\n82\t        AE003[\"A-E003 license-validation\"]\n83\t        AE004[\"A-E004 konflux-onboarding\"]\n84\t        AE005[\"A-E005 managed store via Platform Operator\"]\n85\t        AE006[\"A-E006 Llama Stack managed provider\"]\n86\t        AE007[\"A-E007 Dashboard deploy-and-manage UI\"]\n87\t        AE008[\"A-E008 managed-posture docs\"]\n88\t    end\n89\t\n90\t    subgraph BranchB[\"Branch B — external-connection-only\"]\n91\t        BE002[\"B-E002 Dashboard connection registration + UI\"]\n92\t        BE003[\"B-E003 Llama Stack external provider\"]\n93\t        BE004[\"B-E004 external-posture docs\"]\n94\t    end\n95\t\n96\t    E001 --> AE002\n97\t    E001 --> AE003\n98\t    E001 --> AE005\n99\t    E001 --> AE006\n100\t    E001 --> AE007\n101\t    E001 --> AE008\n102\t    AE002 --> AE004\n103\t    AE003 --> AE005\n104\t    AE005 --> AE006\n105\t    AE005 --> AE007\n106\t    AE002 --> AE008\n107\t    AE003 --> AE008\n108\t    AE004 --> AE008\n109\t    AE005 --> AE008\n110\t    AE006 --> AE008\n111\t    AE007 --> AE008\n112\t\n113\t    E001 --> BE002\n114\t    E001 --> BE003\n115\t    E001 --> BE004\n116\t    BE002 --> BE003\n117\t    BE002 --> BE004\n118\t    BE003 --> BE004\n119\t```\n120\t\n121\tNote: BRANCH-A-E004 (konflux-onboarding) deliberately has **no** edge to\n122\tBRANCH-A-E005 — they run in parallel (Rule 10); the coupling is enforced by the\n123\t\"build pipeline green\" acceptance criterion on BRANCH-A-E005 (Rule 25).\n124\t\n125\t## DAG Justification\n126\t\n127\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n128\t|------|------|-----------|\n129\t| E001 → all branch epics | 3 | Posture decision determines existence and shape of all HLR-2..HLR-5 work; true gate with `remove` impact — the non-selected branch does not exist. Bounded (2 outcomes) → conditional branches. |\n130\t| BRANCH-A-E002 → BRANCH-A-E004 | 5 | repo-onboarding → konflux-onboarding always serial; the pipeline needs the repo. |\n131\t| BRANCH-A-E003 → BRANCH-A-E005 | 9 | license-validation → general implementation serial; license failures would change the dependency set and approach. |\n132\t| (none) BRANCH-A-E004 → BRANCH-A-E005 | 10 | konflux-onboarding ∥ general implementation parallel by rule; enforced via \"build pipeline green\" AC on A-E005 (Rule 25), never a DAG edge. |\n133\t| BRANCH-A-E005 → BRANCH-A-E006 | 12/13 | The Llama Stack provider wiring builds against the managed-store interface the Platform Operator work produces (framework → consumer serial). |\n134\t| BRANCH-A-E005 → BRANCH-A-E007 | 13 | The Dashboard integration builds against the managed vector store resource/interface produced by the operator work (Dashboard reads/writes CRs per architecture context). |\n135\t| BRANCH-A-E006 ∥ BRANCH-A-E007 | 14 | Different components/repos (Llama Stack vs Dashboard), no shared artifacts — parallel. |\n136\t| BRANCH-A-E002/E003/E004/E005/E006/E007 → BRANCH-A-E008 | 11 | docs-authoring blocked by ALL Implementation epics; docs describe what was built. |\n137\t| BRANCH-B-E002 → BRANCH-B-E003 | 13 | The Llama Stack wiring consumes the connection registration format/artifact the Dashboard work produces (build-against artifact, not a mere config reference — \"without hand-editing distribution configs\" means the wiring integrates with the registration mechanism). |\n138\t| BRANCH-B-E002, BRANCH-B-E003 → BRANCH-B-E004 | 11 | docs-authoring blocked by all Implementation epics in the branch. |\n139\t| BRANCH-A-E002 ∥ BRANCH-A-E003 | 7 | repo-onboarding and license-validation have independent inputs — parallel. |\n140\t| BRANCH-A-E003 ∥ BRANCH-A-E004 | 8 | license-validation and konflux-onboarding parallel; config does not depend on specific deps. |\n141\t\n142\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n143\t\n144\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n145\t|-----|----------|---------|\n146\t| HLR-1 (posture decision with documented evaluation) | P0 | E001 |\n147\t| HLR-2 (managed deployment OR external connection registration) | P0 | Branch A: BRANCH-A-E005 · Branch B: BRANCH-B-E002 |\n148\t| HLR-3 (Llama Stack consumes chosen store as RAG provider) | P1 | Branch A: BRANCH-A-E006 · Branch B: BRANCH-B-E003 |\n149\t| HLR-4 (Dashboard exposes chosen integration) | P1 | Branch A: BRANCH-A-E007 · Branch B: BRANCH-B-E002 (consolidated, see below) |\n150\t| HLR-5 (documentation for shipped posture) | P2 | Branch A: BRANCH-A-E008 · Branch B: BRANCH-B-E004 |\n151\t\n152\tFull coverage: every HLR maps to at least one epic in **each** branch, so\n153\teither investigation outcome satisfies the strategy.\n154\t\n155\t**HLR consolidation note (Branch B):** HLR-2 (external path: \"data scientists\n156\tcan register an external vector store connection per project with validated\n157\tconnectivity\") and HLR-4 (external path: \"connection management UI\") describe\n158\tthe same Dashboard surface for the same component and team — HLR-4 is the\n159\tpresentation mode of HLR-2's registration capability. Per the\n160\tredundant/overlapping HLR rule they are ONE capability covered by a single epic\n161\t(BRANCH-B-E002) at the higher priority (P0). In Branch A they remain separate\n162\tepics because they land on different components (Platform Operator vs\n163\tDashboard).\n164\t\n165\t## Component and Onboarding Notes\n166\t\n167\t- All three affected components (Llama Stack, Dashboard, Platform Operator) are\n168\t  canonical RHAI Jira components — no non-obvious mappings were needed.\n169\t- **Platform Operator has no architecture-context file**, so per the\n170\t  in-platform rule it is treated as not yet in the platform: the managed branch\n171\t  (Branch A — the only branch that changes Platform Operator code) carries the\n172\t  full Rule 20 onboarding chain: repo-onboarding + license-validation (parallel\n173\t  start) → konflux-onboarding (after repo) → general implementation (after\n174\t  license-validation, parallel with konflux-onboarding). Branch B touches no\n175\t  Platform Operator code and therefore needs no onboarding.\n176\t- Llama Stack and Dashboard both have architecture-context files (in platform,\n177\t  Konflux-onboarded) — no onboarding epics generated for them.\n178\t- Milvus itself is not a RHAI Jira component; work to deploy and productize it\n179\t  is assigned to the Platform Operator canonical component as the most-relevant\n180\t  name.\n181\t\n182\t## Health Warnings\n183\t\n184\t1. **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): the strategy has no Impacted\n185\t   Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria,\n186\t   and no Non-Functional Requirements. Epic acceptance criteria were derived\n187\t   from the HLRs and problem statement; team assignments were taken from\n188\t   architecture context where available.\n189\t2. **Assumed team assignments**: no owner is stated for Platform Operator\n190\t   (assigned \"RHOAI Platform\") or Documentation (assigned \"RHOAI\n191\t   Documentation\") — verify with the strategy owner before execution.\n192\t3. **Platform Operator in-platform status**: the onboarding chain in Branch A\n193\t   rests on Platform Operator lacking an architecture-context file. If the\n194\t   operator already ships in the platform (context file simply missing