id routing: \"Medium → hybrid (skill resolves the desk/local parts and hands a spec to a human for the rest)\"). The routing tiers explicitly anticipate this combination! Medium = hybrid. So source_accessibility: 1 + human_judgment_required: -2 → the skill does the desk research, human decides. That's a coherent, rubric-sanctioned combination.\n\nWait, but then what does the review prompt's example mean — \"an Investigation marking source_accessibility: 1 when the answer is a pending human decision\"? That's for cases where the answer comes ONLY from the pending decision — no readable material holds or determines it. E.g., \"will product approve X?\" Here, the evaluation question has a substantial readable evidence base.\n\nOK here's my final judgment: the value is defensible but borderline. The strategy's own open question says the decision is \"genuinely undecided\" and the answer \"changes most of the downstream work\" — a product call. The rationale for source_accessibility: 1 conflates input readability with answer determinability. I'll score Criterion 5: 1 with a minor issue (borderline source_accessibility). Per scoring rules, minor issues alone don't reduce score... wait: \"Minor issues alone do not reduce the score, but 3+ minors in the same criterion costs 1 point.\" But Criterion 5's rubric itself says score 1 when \"1-2 have arguable values (e.g., a borderline source_accessibility for an Investigation)\". The criterion-specific rubric governs: 1 point for borderline values. So Criterion 5 = 1.\n\nNow let me also double check a couple of implementation signal values against the 9-signal rubric. Let me read the implementation signals rubric portion (lines ~175-192).","signature":"d901f75ebe5c4f66a92887fd7eb16420"}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":41109,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9675dbf1e0526aff","agent_id":"aa6cd019c2423aa9a","session_id":