figs\" means the wiring integrates with the registration mechanism). |\n137\t| BRANCH-B-E002, BRANCH-B-E003 → BRANCH-B-E004 | 11 | docs-authoring blocked by all Implementation epics in the branch. |\n138\t| BRANCH-A-E002 ∥ BRANCH-A-E003 | 7 | repo-onboarding and license-validation have independent inputs — parallel. |\n139\t| BRANCH-A-E003 ∥ BRANCH-A-E004 | 8 | license-validation and konflux-onboarding parallel; config does not depend on specific deps. |\n140\t\n141\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n142\t\n143\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n144\t|-----|----------|---------|\n145\t| HLR-1 (posture decision with documented evaluation) | P0 | E001 |\n146\t| HLR-2 (managed deployment OR external connection registration) | P0 | Branch A: BRANCH-A-E005 · Branch B: BRANCH-B-E002 |\n147\t| HLR-3 (Llama Stack consumes chosen store as RAG provider) | P1 | Branch A: BRANCH-A-E006 · Branch B: BRANCH-B-E003 |\n148\t| HLR-4 (Dashboard exposes chosen integration) | P1 | Branch A: BRANCH-A-E007 · Branch B: BRANCH-B-E002 (consolidated, see below) |\n149\t| HLR-5 (documentation for shipped posture) | P2 | Branch A: BRANCH-A-E008 · Branch B: BRANCH-B-E004 |\n150\t\n151\tFull coverage: every HLR maps to at least one epic in **each** branch, so\n152\teither investigation outcome satisfies the strategy.\n153\t\n154\t**HLR consolidation note (Branch B):** HLR-2 (external path: \"data scientists\n155\tcan register an external vector store connection per project with validated\n156\tconnectivity\") and HLR-4 (external path: \"connection management UI\") describe\n157\tthe same Dashboard surface for the same component and team — HLR-4 is the\n158\tpresentation mode of HLR-2's registration capability. Per the\n159\tredundant/overlapping HLR rule they are ONE capability covered by a single epic\n160\t(BRANCH-B-E002) at the higher priority (P0). In Branch A they remain separate\n161\tepics because they land on different components (Platform Operator vs\n162\tDashboard).\n163\t\n164\t## Component and