119\t```\n120\t\n121\tNote: BRANCH-A-E004 (konflux-onboarding) deliberately has **no** edge to\n122\tBRANCH-A-E005 — they run in parallel (Rule 10); the coupling is enforced by the\n123\t\"build pipeline green\" acceptance criterion on BRANCH-A-E005 (Rule 25).\n124\t\n125\t## DAG Justification\n126\t\n127\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n128\t|------|------|-----------|\n129\t| E001 → all branch epics | 3 | Posture decision determines existence and shape of all HLR-2..HLR-5 work; true gate with `remove` impact — the non-selected branch does not exist. Bounded (2 outcomes) → conditional branches. |\n130\t| BRANCH-A-E002 → BRANCH-A-E004 | 5 | repo-onboarding → konflux-onboarding always serial; the pipeline needs the repo. |\n131\t| BRANCH-A-E002 → BRANCH-A-E005 | 6 | repo-onboarding → general implementation of the onboarded component always serial; the operator code for the managed vector store lands in the midstream repository A-E002 creates. (Rule 10 exempts only the konflux-onboarding ↔ general pairing, not this edge.) |\n132\t| BRANCH-A-E003 → BRANCH-A-E005 | 9 | license-validation → general implementation serial; license failures would change the dependency set and approach. |\n133\t| (none) BRANCH-A-E004 → BRANCH-A-E005 | 10 | konflux-onboarding ∥ general implementation parallel by rule; enforced via \"build pipeline green\" AC on A-E005 (Rule 25), never a DAG edge. |\n134\t| BRANCH-A-E005 → BRANCH-A-E006 | 12/13 | The Llama Stack provider wiring builds against the managed-store interface the Platform Operator work produces (framework → consumer serial). |\n135\t| BRANCH-A-E005 → BRANCH-A-E007 | 13 | The Dashboard integration builds against the managed vector store resource/interface produced by the operator work (Dashboard reads/writes CRs per architecture context). |\n136\t| BRANCH-A-E006 ∥ BRANCH-A-E007 | 14 | Different components/repos (Llama Stack vs Dashboard), no shared artifacts — parallel. |\n137\t| BRANCH-A-E002/E003/E004/E005/E006/E007 → BRANCH-A-E008 | 11 | docs-authoring blocked by ALL Implementation epics; docs describe what was built. |\n138\t| BRANCH-B-E002 → BRANCH-B-E003 | 13 | The Llama Stack wiring consumes the connection registration format/artifact the Dashboard work produces (build-against artifact, not a mere config reference — \"without hand-editing distribution configs\" means the wiring integrates with the registration mechanism). |\n139\t| BRANCH-B-E002, BRANCH-B-E003 → BRANCH-B-E004 | 11 | docs-authoring blocked by all Implementation epics in the branch. |\n140\t| BRANCH-A-E002 ∥ BRANCH-A-E003 | 7 | repo-onboarding and license-validation have independent inputs — parallel. |\n141\t| BRANCH-A-E003 ∥ BRANCH-A-E004 | 8 | license-validation and konflux-onboarding parallel; config does not depend on specific deps. |\n142\t\n143\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n144\t\n145\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n146\t|-----|----------|---------|\n147\t| HLR-1 (posture decision with documented evaluation) | P0 | E001 |\n148\t| HLR-2 (managed deployment OR external connection registration) | P0 | Branch A: BRANCH-A-E005 · Branch B: BRANCH-B-E002 |\n149\t| HLR-3 (Llama Stack consumes chosen store as RAG provider) | P1 | Branch A: BRANCH-A-E006 · Branch B: BRANCH-B-E003 |\n150\t| HLR-4 (Dashboard exposes chosen integration) | P1 | Branch A: BRANCH-A-E007 · Branch B: BRANCH-B-E002 (consolidated, see below) |\n151\t| HLR-5 (documentation for shipped posture) | P2 | Branch A: BRANCH-A-E008 · Branch B: BRANCH-B-E004 |\n152\t\n153\tFull coverage: every HLR maps to at least one epic in **each** branch, so\n154\teither investigation outcome satisfies the strategy.\n155\t\n156\t**HLR consolidation note (Branch B):** HLR-2 (external path: \"data scientists\n157\tcan register an external vector store connection per project with validated\n158\tconnectivity\") and HLR-4 (external path: \"connection management UI\") describe\n159\tthe same Dashboard surface for the same component and team — HLR-4 is the\n160\tpresentation mode of HLR-2's registration capability. Per the\n161\tredundant/overlapping HLR rule they are ONE capability covered by a single epic\n162\t(BRANCH-B-E002) at the higher priority (P0). In Branch A they remain separate\n163\tepics because they land on different components (Platform Operator vs\n164\tDashboard).\n165\t\n166\t## Component and Onboarding Notes\n167\t\n168\t- All three affected components (Llama Stack, Dashboard, Platform Operator) are\n169\t  canonical RHAI Jira components — no non-obvious mappings were needed.\n170\t- **Platform Operator has no architecture-context file**, so per the\n171\t  in-platform rule it is treated as not yet in the platform: the managed branch\n172\t  (Branch A — the only branch that changes Platform Operator code) carries the\n173\t  full Rule 20 onboarding chain: repo-onboarding + license-validation (parallel\n174\t  start) → konflux-onboarding (after repo) → general implementation (after\n175\t  repo-onboarding and license-validation, parallel with konflux-onboarding). Branch B touches no\n176\t  Platform Operator code and therefore needs no onboarding.\n177\t- Llama Stack and Dashboard both have architecture-context files (in platform,\n178\t  Konflux-onboarded) — no onboarding epics generated for them.\n179\t- Milvus itself is not a RHAI Jira component; work to deploy and productize it\n180\t  is assigned to the Platform Operator canonical component as the most-relevant\n181\t  name.\n182\t\n183\t## Health Warnings\n184\t\n185\t1. **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): the strategy has no Impacted\n186\t   Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria,\n187\t   and no Non-Functional Requirements. Epic acceptance criteria were derived\n188\t   from the HLRs and problem statement; team assignments were taken from\n189\t   architecture context where available.\n190\t2. **Assumed team assignments**: no owner is stated for Platform Operator\n191\t   (assigned \"RHOAI Platform\") or Documentation (assigned \"RHOAI\n192\t   Documentation\") — verify with the strategy owner before execution.\n193\t3. **Platform Operator in-platform status**: the onboarding chain in Branch A\n194\t   rests on Platform Operator lacking an architecture-context file. If the\n195\t   operator already ships in the platform (context file simply missing), the\n196\t   onboarding chain collapses into the general implementation epic.\n197\t4. **Scope trap — \"productized images\"**: HLR-2's productized-images clause\n198\t   implies image build/release infrastructure for Milvus. This is folded into\n199\t   BRANCH-A-E005's scope; if a separate midstream image repository turns out to\n200\t   be required, split it out at implementation planning time.\n201\t5. **Docs priority exception applied**: the docs epics are P1 (strategy\n202\t   frontmatter priority Major → P1) even though HLR-5 is labeled P2. This is\n203\t   the rule-mandated docs-authoring priority exception, not an inconsistency —\n204\t   flagged for human verification.\n205\t6. **No priority inversions detected**: the onboarding chain is P0 because it\n206\t   exists solely to enable the P0 HLR-2 operator implementation.\n207\t\n208\t## Tiered Delivery\n209\t\n210\tNot applicable. The fork here is an internal product decision with bounded\n211\toutcomes (modeled as conditional branches), not an external dependency with\n212\tuncertain timing. Shipping both postures in the same release is explicitly out\n213\tof scope, so no partial-value tiering across postures is offered; within each\n214\tbranch, P1 epics (Llama Stack wiring, Dashboard UI) are independently\n215\tdeferrable features behind the P0 core.\n216\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b6f2b18ba21e4e42","agent_id":"a3c9735196fff5c3d","session_id":"124e4662-1fae-4b45-93d6-fde3aa562cb7","uuid":"db87187a-61e0-4cc3-b24a-4ed355d244c5","timestamp":"2026-10-22T14:11:43.885Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b893e9aba458e232","permission_decision":{"decision":"acce